## Medicines

ADHD medicine can help with concentration, impulsiveness and organisation. It does not cure ADHD, but many people find it makes a big difference.

- **Methylphenidate** and **lisdexamfetamine** are stimulant medicines and are usually tried first.
- **Atomoxetine** and **guanfacine** are non-stimulant options if stimulants do not work or are not suitable.

Your specialist will start with a low dose and adjust it. Once your dose is stable, your GP may take over prescribing under a **shared care agreement**.

**Common side effects** include reduced appetite, trouble sleeping, headaches and a raised heart rate. You will have regular checks of your **weight, heart rate and blood pressure**.

## Support and therapy

- **Psychoeducation:** learning about ADHD and how it affects you
- **CBT** adapted for ADHD, which can help with organisation, procrastination and low self-esteem
- **Coaching**, to build practical systems for daily life
- **Parent training programmes** for parents of children with ADHD
- support at **school**, such as extra time or a quieter space

## Reviews

You should have a **review at least once a year** to check that treatment is still helping and whether anything needs to change.

{% callout type="warn" title="Medication shortages" %}
If you cannot get your ADHD medicine because of a shortage, speak to your pharmacist and prescriber. Do not share or buy medicines from anywhere other than a pharmacy.
{% /callout %}

